title: LSP Completion

The editor offers tiddler titles while you type a link or a transclusion, and names wherever ~TiddlyWiki expects one, through `textDocument/completion`. Try it in [[LSP Completion - (Example)]].

!! How a title is matched

Titles are offered only inside an unclosed `[[` or `{{`.

Matching is by ''prefix'' and ignores case. At least two characters are needed: one letter matches too much of any wiki to be a useful list.

A title is offered when either holds, best first:

# the title ''starts with'' everything you typed
# every ''word'' you typed starts a word of the title

The second rule is why `LS S` finds `LSP Server`, and why the list survives a space: a space begins the next word rather than narrowing the search to a literal that no title contains. A title's words are split on `_`, `-`, `/`, `:` and `.` as well as spaces, so `ed` reaches `LSP Editor Setup`.

Nothing matches on its middle. Typing `dit` does not offer `LSP Editor Setup`, because a title is not looked for by its interior.

System titles are left out until what you typed starts with `$:/`, which also reaches shadow tiddlers. At most 200 titles come back at once.

The list is always reported as incomplete, so the editor asks again on every keystroke instead of caching it and filtering on its own. An editor filtering locally stops at the space in a title, which would hide exactly the multi-word titles this exists for.

!! Names

Where ~TiddlyWiki expects a name, the names it would reach from there are offered, with no minimum length:

|! Typed |! Offered |
|`<<`, or a `$variable=` or `$name=` value |names in reach, in the order ~TiddlyWiki looks: what an enclosing definition or widget binds, this tiddler's definitions, globals, ~JavaScript macros, then the variables ~TiddlyWiki sets |
|inside a call, after a space |the parameters not given yet, by name or by position, as `tag:` in `<<call>>` or `tag=` in a widget |
|`<$` |custom widgets in reach, then every registered widget |
|where a step starts in a filter |filter operators, then dotted functions in reach |
|`[<` in a filter |variables and functions in reach |
|`[function[` |functions in reach |

A filter is recognised on its line: a filter attribute, a `{{{ }}}`, an `<%if%>` condition or a `\function` body. A call is read back across lines, so a `<$macrocall` spread over five lines is still offered its parameters. A literal operand, prose and a closed call offer nothing.

The list opens by itself on `[`, `{`, `<`, `$`, `"`, `=` and a space, and follows the same rules as titles: always incomplete, ranked by the server.
